<edge source="rus_ukr_war::logistics_capacity" target="china_eu_tech_rivalry::ai_asset_price_volatilit" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.8</data>
  <data key="d37">Degraded logistics and transport capacity causes sudden shortages and delivery delays for semiconductors and AI hardware, producing rapid repricing and higher short‑term volatility in equities and suppliers tied to AI supply chains.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">rus_ukr_war</data>
  <data key="d40">china_eu_tech_rivalry</data>
</edge>
<edge source="rus_ukr_war::logistics_capacity" target="opec_energy_volatility::maritime_transit_risk" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.65</data>
  <data key="d37">Improved logistics and sustainment capacity (e.g., mine‑clearing, convoy escorting, port repair) enables sustained demining and port operations that reduce the likelihood and duration of shipping disruptions and thus lower maritime transit risk.</data>
  <data key="d38">6-18 months</data>
  <data key="d39">rus_ukr_war</data>
  <data key="d40">opec_energy_volatility</data>
</edge>
<edge source="rus_ukr_war::logistics_capacity" target="us_iran_confront::crude_supply_gap" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.65</data>
  <data key="d37">Degradation of logistics and export corridor capacity (ports, rail, pipeline throughput) in the rus_ukr_war reduces volumes reaching world markets and thus widens an immediate crude supply gap; conversely restored capacity narrows that gap.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">rus_ukr_war</data>
  <data key="d40">us_iran_confront</data>
</edge>
