  <data key="d37">Higher risk to shipping (attacks, seizures, interdiction) drives up insurance and forces carriers to avoid Black Sea and nearby ports, immediately reducing inbound cargo volumes, port throughput and the region's ability to resupply forces and civil stocks.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">opec_energy_volatility</data>
  <data key="d40">rus_ukr_war</data>
</edge>
<edge source="opec_energy_volatility::maritime_transit_risk" target="rus_ukr_war::arms_supply_flow" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">-</data>
  <data key="d29">2</data>
  <data key="d36">0.7</data>
  <data key="d37">Increased danger to sea routes raises delivery costs and delays, prompts some suppliers to pause or reroute shipments of heavy weapons and spare parts, and shrinks the effective external arms deliveries into the conflict zone.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">opec_energy_volatility</data>
  <data key="d40">rus_ukr_war</data>
</edge>
<edge source="opec_energy_volatility::maritime_transit_risk" target="us_iran_confront::mil_escalation_threat" id="0">
  <data key="d28">cross_causal</data>
  <data key="d34">+</data>
  <data key="d29">3</data>
  <data key="d36">0.75</data>
  <data key="d37">A rising likelihood of attacks, seizures, or interdictions in key sea lanes leads the US to mount more aggressive protective operations and preemptive countermeasures against actors blamed for the threats, increasing escalation risk.</data>
  <data key="d38">0-6 months</data>
  <data key="d39">opec_energy_volatility</data>
  <data key="d40">us_iran_confront</data>
</edge>
